A game has n planets, connected by m teleporters. Two planets a and b belong to the same kingdom exactly when there is a route both from a to b and from b to a. Your task is to determine for each planet its kingdom.

Input:
The first input line has two integers n and m: the number of planets and teleporters. The planets are numbered 1,2,...,n.
After this, there are m lines describing the teleporters. Each line has two integers a and b: you can travel from planet a to planet b through a teleporter.

Output:
First print an integer k: the number of kingdoms. After this, print for each planet a kingdom label between 1 and k. You can print any valid solution.

Constraints:
1<=n<=10^5
1<=m<=2*10^5
1<=a,b<=n

Strategy:
We can use Kosaraju's algorithm to find the strongly connected components. We do this by running it as a dfs and in the first pass we just visit the nodes and when the stacks start collapsing, at the end of the recursive function we put them in a stack. Since SCCs are basically cycles or single nodes in a graph and they are connected unidirectionally to other SCCs we put them in the stack in an order in which, when we run the reverse pass, we will extract them so they cannot bleed into each other. Meaning, in the order so the edges between the components we are processing point to already processed components or are not there at all. This is the definition of topological sort which would guarantee we traverse all the components but the graph is reversed so we traverse none. The first pass basically sorts the nodes into SCCs and sorts these topologically. Then we take take and run the reverse dfs in which we just extract the things from the stack, let dfs rip and mark the whole cycle (SCC) and then move onto the next one while indexing them. At the end we just output the highest index and the contents of the indexing array.